<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:media="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Media;assembly=PresentationCore">

    <!--
        WHAT THE LIST IS BUILT FROM. The grid, the row and the template it owns, and the
        headings. Not one value of its own - every number, colour and thickness below is a
        name declared in Values.xaml.

        WHAT A CELL IS AND WHAT IT SHOWS MOVED TO Cells.xaml ON 2026-08-12 - the cell, the
        four ways a word is written in one, the two marks and the two templated cells. The
        seam is between what a person READS and what the list is MADE OF, and it was cut
        because this file stood exactly on the 507-line markup ceiling while backlog 166
        needed more lines to repair the start mark.

        ADR-23, the same rule as the other three files: a value invented here would be as
        invisible as one invented in a view, and the guard treats all four alike.

        SPLIT OUT OF Controls.xaml ON 2026-08-11 WITH NOTHING REWRITTEN - the styles below
        are the ones that were there, in the order they were in. Controls.xaml stood exactly
        on its own ceiling of 530 lines, which refused the two cell templates the
        configurable columns need, and backlog 163 had already measured where the seam was.

        WHY THE NAME IS List RATHER THAN THE Text BACKLOG 163 PROPOSED: the other half holds
        TextBox and CheckBox, which are not text. Naming each half after what is in it beat
        naming it after which one moved.

        NONE OF THE FOUR LOADS ON ITS OWN, AND THIS ONE FAILS TWICE OVER. It reaches WPF
        UI's keys - DefaultDataGridStyle, DefaultDataGridRowStyle and the rest - and it
        reaches ours in Values.xaml, and StaticResource resolves both WHILE THE FILE IS BEING
        READ. So the merge order in App.xaml is the library's dictionaries, then Values.xaml,
        then Controls.xaml, then this, then Cells.xaml. Anything that loads these files
        elsewhere has to do the same, which is why WpfHost and tools/wpfui-probe both build
        the stack in that order.

        NO LONGER LAST OF OURS - Cells.xaml is, since 2026-08-12, and the reason that
        direction exists is unchanged: an implicit style merged later wins, so the more
        specialised half sits on top. Nothing in either is implicit today, so the order costs
        nothing now and is the one that will still be right the day something is.
    -->
    <!--
        Default look per control type, so a control is consistent without anybody remembering
        to apply a style. The guard checks these too: every Value below points at a name.
    -->
    <!--
        THE THREE ROW STATES LIVE ON THE ROW, AND THAT MOVED HERE FROM THE CELL ON 2026-08-02.
        Read the next four paragraphs before touching either style - this exact question has
        now been got wrong twice, in opposite directions, and both times silently.

        Under the built-in Fluent theme the cell painted an OPAQUE background and covered the
        row, so a Background setter on DataGridRow reached nothing. Three states sat there,
        exactly one appeared, and it only appeared because the same brush was also on the cell.
        Fourteen frames of a service stopping, and the declared #3A3320 never showed once.

        Under WPF UI it is the other way round, and this is read out of their markup rather
        than guessed: DefaultDataGridRowStyle templates its border as
        Background="{TemplateBinding Background}", while DefaultDataGridCellStyle hardcodes
        Background="Transparent" on its own border and never binds the property at all. So a
        Background setter on the CELL is now the dead one.

        THE PARAGRAPH THAT USED TO FOLLOW SAID WPF UI SUPPLIES NO FEEDBACK TO LEAN ON, BECAUSE
        EVERY VisualState IN THEIR TEMPLATES IS EMPTY. The VisualStates are indeed empty and
        that half was right. The conclusion was wrong, and it was wrong in the direction that
        cost the most: their feedback lives in ControlTemplate.Triggers, not in visual states,
        and it was drawing hover and selection all along - in THEIR brush, over ours, with the
        two states sharing one colour so that they could not be told apart. Whoever reads a
        template for this again: read the triggers, not only the states.

        None of it applies to the row any more, because the row no longer uses their template.
        The cell still does.

        RowStyle and CellStyle are set explicitly on the DataGrid in MainWindow.xaml rather
        than left to implicit lookup. Their DefaultDataGridStyle sets both properties itself,
        and an explicit setter on the grid beats an implicit style - so leaving these keyed but
        unreferenced would be a third way to make the same mistake.

        tools/gui-probe/check.ps1 is the only instrument that can tell whether any of this
        reaches a pixel. A property read says #3A3320 either way.
    -->
    <!--
        The grid itself, and it exists for one setter that is not optional.

        DataGridTextColumn TRANSFERS FontSize from the grid onto every TextBlock it generates,
        as a LOCAL value - and a local value beats a default style. So the implicit TextBlock
        style below, which carries our size, loses inside every cell of this list. Their
        DefaultDataGridStyle sets FontSize to their own DefaultDataGridFontSize, and the first
        build after the migration came out with rows a third taller and the PID column clipped
        to two digits, because the wider text pushed the automatic columns past the viewport.

        Neither symptom looked like a font size, and that is why this comment is here.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ListGrid" TargetType="DataGrid" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultDataGridStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeNormal}" />
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        THE ROW OWNS ITS OWN TEMPLATE SINCE 2026-08-05, AND THAT IS THE FIX FOR A FAMILY OF
        FAULTS RATHER THAN FOR ONE OF THEM. Read this before moving a state back into a style
        trigger, because that is the shape that failed.

        What was wrong: the four states were setters on DataGridRow.Background inside this
        style. Their template feeds that property to its border through a TemplateBinding, so
        it looks like it should arrive - and it never did. Their template also carries a
        trigger on ItemsControl.AlternationIndex = 0 that sets the same border's Background
        from the grid's RowBackground, and AlternationIndex is zero for every row while
        AlternationCount is zero, so it is on for every row all the time. Inside a template,
        a trigger's setter outranks a TemplateBinding on the same element. Our colour lost that
        contest on every row, every frame, in silence.

        This is the FIFTH time in this product that XAML bound correctly and painted something
        else, after the empty list from internal members, column headers outside the visual
        tree, the cell painting over the row under the built-in theme, and the white search
        box. Rule 7 of the project notes says a problem that keeps coming back is a wrong
        primitive rather than a missing tweak. The wrong primitive was borrowing a template and
        arguing with it from outside.

        WHAT WE GIVE UP BY OWNING IT: their template's future improvements, and about forty
        lines to maintain. WHAT WE GET: four states that are template triggers, which is the
        one place nothing can outrank, plus a zebra stripe that becomes possible again - the
        two AlternationIndex triggers below are kept and come FIRST, so setting
        AlternatingRowBackground on the grid would stripe the list without touching any state.
        Backlog 123 asks for exactly that and could not have it while the states were dying.

        STILL NOT PROVEN AT THE PIXEL. This explains a measurement rather than replacing one.
        tools/gui-probe/check.ps1 is the instrument, and it needs a person.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ListRow" TargetType="DataGridRow" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultDataGridRowStyle}">
        <!--
            Margin and padding pulled back to nothing, against their 0,2 and 4. The owner chose
            to keep this list's density on 2026-08-02: their spacing costs about 17% of the
            rows on screen, measured twice, and this window exists to show 810 services.
        -->
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource MarginNone}" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource MarginNone}" />

        <!--
            THE ROW IS THE HIT SURFACE, AND SINCE 2026-08-31 IT IS A SETTER RATHER THAN A TRIGGER.
            What this line does is what the AlternationIndex=0 trigger below used to do through
            TargetNullValue: give RowSurface a brush, because a null background takes no mouse hits
            and the row would be a hole. Measured before that repair with row-cost.ps1: 106 of 603
            points sampled across a row landed on a cell border rather than on the row. Backlog 221.

            WHY IT MOVED. The trigger carried a Binding with RelativeSource FindAncestor, so every
            realised row walked the visual tree up to the DataGrid - on realisation and again on
            every recycle - to arrive at the same answer every time. A Setter needs no walk and no
            binding at all, and the template already feeds Background to RowSurface.

            WHAT IT COSTS: the grid's own RowBackground property stops reaching the row. Nothing sets
            it, and a zebra stripe arrives through AlternatingRowBackground and the trigger below,
            which is what backlog 123 asks for - so this takes nothing away that was in use. Setting
            RowBackground on the grid after this line would silently do nothing, and that is the one
            thing somebody could get wrong here.
        -->
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}" />

        <Setter Property="Template">
            <Setter.Value>
                <ControlTemplate TargetType="DataGridRow">
                    <Border x:Name="RowSurface"
                            Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                            SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
                        <Grid>
                            <!--
                                THREE THINGS THIS TEMPLATE USED TO BUILD LEFT ON 2026-08-24, AND ALL
                                THREE WERE PROVABLY DEAD RATHER THAN PROBABLY UNUSED. Counted before
                                and after with tools/gui-probe/row-cost.ps1, backlog 221.

                                A `DataGridRowHeader`, WHICH THIS GRID CAN NEVER SHOW: MainWindow.xaml
                                sets HeadersVisibility="Column", so the header was built for every
                                realised row and then collapsed by its own binding - and that binding
                                was a FindAncestor walk up to the grid, paid once per row on every
                                recycle, to arrive at the same answer every time.

                                A `DataGridDetailsPresenter`, for a panel under the row. No
                                RowDetailsTemplate exists anywhere in this product, so it presented
                                nothing to nobody.

                                A `SelectiveScrollingGrid` with two columns and two rows, whose entire
                                job is holding those two in place while the list scrolls sideways.
                                With neither of them left it was a two-by-two layout around a single
                                child.

                                WHAT BRINGS EACH BACK, so that this reads as a decision rather than a
                                loss: row headers need the header and the selective scrolling grid
                                again, and a detail panel needs the presenter and the second row
                                definition. Either is a deliberate change with a look of its own, not
                                a line to restore quietly.
                            -->
                            <DataGridCellsPresenter
                                ItemsPanel="{TemplateBinding ItemsPanel}"
                                SnapsToDevicePixels="{TemplateBinding SnapsToDevicePixels}" />

                            <!--
                                A sibling rather than a border on the row, so it costs no
                                layout - an empty Border asks for two pixels and the cells
                                beside it ask for more, so the row height is unchanged. Not
                                hit testable, or the ring would swallow clicks on its own row.
                            -->
                            <Border x:Name="FocusOutline"
                                    BorderBrush="{StaticResource FocusRing}"
                                    BorderThickness="{StaticResource FocusRingThickness}"
                                    Visibility="Collapsed"
                                    IsHitTestVisible="False"
                                    SnapsToDevicePixels="True" />
                        </Grid>
                    </Border>

                    <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                        <!--
                            The grid's own two, kept and kept FIRST. They are what a zebra stripe
                            would come back through - underneath the states rather than over them,
                            which is the arrangement backlog 123 needs.

                            THEY WERE DESCRIBED HERE AS "INERT WHILE THE GRID SETS NEITHER BRUSH"
                            AND THAT WAS WRONG IN A WAY THAT MATTERED. The grid sets neither, so
                            this first trigger was reaching in and setting the row's Background to
                            NULL on every row, every frame - and a null background takes no mouse
                            hits. The row surface was not inert, it was a hole, and the only reason
                            hover and selection worked at all is that WPF UI's cell template paints
                            a fully transparent border which DOES take hits. Twenty of those per
                            row were doing the work of one.

                            TargetNullValue is what makes the row itself the hit surface. Measured
                            with tools/gui-probe/row-cost.ps1 before the change: of 603 points
                            sampled across a row, 106 landed on a cell border rather than on text -
                            so dropping that border without this line would have punched holes
                            through a sixth of every row. Backlog 221.

                            It costs backlog 123 nothing: a real RowBackground still wins, because
                            TargetNullValue only speaks when the source is null.
                        -->
                        <Trigger Property="ItemsControl.AlternationIndex" Value="1">
                            <Setter TargetName="RowSurface"
                                    Property="Background"
                                    Value="{Binding AlternatingRowBackground,
                                        RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type DataGrid}},
                                        TargetNullValue={x:Static media:Brushes.Transparent}}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <!--
                            First of the three colours, so that pointing at a row or selecting
                            it still wins. A person's own hand beats a notification about
                            something that already happened.
                        -->
                        <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding RecentlyChanged}" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="RowSurface"
                                    Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceChanged}" />
                        </DataTrigger>

                        <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="RowSurface"
                                    Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHover}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <!--
                            LAST of the three, so selection beats the pointer, and that order is
                            measured rather than assumed. With the pointer winning, a row went
                            grey the instant it was clicked and stayed grey for as long as the
                            hand did not move - so the one row the person had just chosen was
                            the one row that did not look chosen.

                            Foreground goes on the row rather than the border, because it has
                            to reach the text inside the cells and it travels by inheritance.
                        -->
                        <Trigger Property="IsSelected" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="RowSurface"
                                    Property="Background"
                                    Value="{StaticResource SurfaceSelected}" />
                            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource TextOnSurface}" />
                        </Trigger>

                        <!--
                            Focus is not one of the three and does not compete with them: it
                            shows an outline and changes no fill, so a row can be selected and
                            focused and still look like both.

                            IsKeyboardFocusWithin rather than IsFocused, because the keyboard
                            lands on a CELL inside the row rather than on the row. Read out of
                            their styles, which set KeyboardNavigation.IsTabStop to False on both
                            the row and the cell, plus how a DataGrid moves focus when the current
                            cell changes. NOT MEASURED at the pixel - that needs an active window,
                            which a test process does not have. Backlog 131.
                        -->
                        <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocusWithin" Value="True">
                            <Setter TargetName="FocusOutline" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                        </Trigger>
                    </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                </ControlTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        The heading, so it stops looking like data. Complaint 4 of the eleven.

        AND IT GIVES WAY WITH AN ELLIPSIS SINCE 2026-08-11, WHICH EVERY CELL HAS DONE SINCE
        2026-08-05 AND NO HEADING EVER HAD. Complaint 6 of the eleven says text cut mid-character
        reads as a rendering fault rather than as "this does not fit", and the fix for it went to
        the cells and stopped there - because with six headings, all of them short, there was
        nothing to see. S6d2 brought eleven more columns and headings like "Against its start
        type" and "Required privileges", and a starred column at its 90 unit floor is narrower
        than either. Photographed on the real window before this setter existed: "Required priv",
        cut in the middle of a word with nothing to say so.

        A ContentTemplate rather than a property, because a heading is a ContentControl and
        TextTrimming belongs to a TextBlock - there is no setter on the header that reaches the
        text. The sort arrow is drawn by their template OUTSIDE this presenter, so it survives.

        The tooltip is the other half of the same rule, exactly as it is for a cell: text that
        gives way has to be gettable. It matters more here than in a cell, because a heading is
        the only place the NAME of what you are looking at appears.
    -->
    <!--
        A RULE AND A STYLE THAT REACHES THE WORDS, 2026-09-25 - owner's complaint that the headings
        look like the rows under them. They did, and for two reasons. Nothing drew a line between
        the names of the columns and the first row. And the size and weight set on this style never
        arrived: the words are a TextBlock in the template below, and the app-wide implicit
        TextBlock style is nearer to it than an inherited value - pitfall 16 of docs/10, in a place
        nobody had looked. A heading now names its own text style and has SurfaceHeadingRule under it.

        NO FILL OF ITS OWN, and that was tried the same day: a band behind the headings was one of
        three zone fills the owner ran and turned down as "many apps in one app". The window keeps
        one surface.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ColumnHeading" TargetType="DataGridColumnHeader" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultDataGridColumnHeaderStyle}">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFace}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource TextSizeHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource TextWeightHeader}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceHeadingRule}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderRuleBelow}" />
        <Setter Property="ContentTemplate">
            <Setter.Value>
                <DataTemplate>
                    <TextBlock Text="{Binding}"
                               Style="{StaticResource ColumnHeadingText}"
                               TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                               ToolTip="{Binding}" />
                </DataTemplate>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--
        A heading over numbers sits where the numbers do, or the column reads as two columns.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="ColumnHeadingNumber" TargetType="DataGridColumnHeader" BasedOn="{StaticResource ColumnHeading}">
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Right" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
